# Mesh Secure Alpha — Implementation Plan

This plan transforms Mesh from a functional P2P prototype into a secure alpha suitable for small private communities. Changes span **five gated milestones** across the Rust backend and React frontend. No milestone is marked complete until its predecessor is verified.

---

## User Review Required

> [!CAUTION]
> **Breaking changes to the invite link format.** Existing v1 invite links (`mesh://join?c=...&k=...`) will stop working. A v1→v2 migration path is included in Milestone 1 (parsing both versions during a transition window), but all newly generated links will be v2.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Yjs authority removal.** The CRDT bridge currently drives channel and community metadata sync. Milestone 2 replaces this with a signed control-log model. The Yjs provider (`yjs-provider.ts`) will be reduced to a local-only collaboration cache or removed entirely. The `y-indexeddb`, `yjs` npm packages and the `crdt_state` table become optional.

> [!WARNING]
> **Voice IPC signature change.** `join_voice`, `leave_voice`, and `send_voice_signal` gain a mandatory `communityId` argument. All frontend voice calls and store types must be updated simultaneously.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Database-at-rest encryption.** Moving from plain `rusqlite` to `sqlcipher` (via the `rusqlite` `bundled-sqlcipher` feature) changes the on-disk database format. A one-time migration will re-encrypt the existing unencrypted database on first launch after the update.

